**user**:
> I have started a plan to build an abstraction around model and tool selection in my AI system. Here is the contract for tools:
> """
> Tool protocol and normalized typed tool-result contract.
> 
> Tools are executable capabilities. In the initial synchronous system they may
> be invoked directly by a responder. In the workflow system they are executed
> as Operations and yield Artifacts. Tool interfaces should therefore avoid
> assuming that their primary result is user-visible text.
> 
> Expected tool callable shape:
> 
> ToolCallable = Callable[
>     [ToolInvocation],
>     ToolResult,
> ]
> 
> @dataclass(frozen=True)
> class ToolInvocation:
>     operation_id: str | None
>     conversation_id: str
>     arguments: Mapping[str, Any]
>     config: Mapping[str, Any]
>     context: PlannerContext | None
>     provenance_requirements: Mapping[str, Any]
> 
> @dataclass
> class Tool:
>     name: str
>     description: str
>     fn: ToolCallable | Callable[..., Any]
>     config: ToolConfig
>     quality_prior: float | None = None
>     cost_estimate: float | None = None
>     version: str = "1"
> 
> Compatibility:
> - registry.py may accept legacy functions such as
>   `fn(prompt, config) -> str`.
> - tools/base.py normalizes such outputs to ToolResult/Artifact.
> - Tool configuration defaults and allowed discrete options are validated here.
> 
> Caller:
> - responders.py or workflow/workers.py invokes:
>       result = tool.fn(invocation)
> - the caller persists raw temporary output as appropriate, converts relevant
>   materials to artifacts/source excerpts, and never blindly injects untrusted
>   tool text into system instructions.
> """
> 
> Now, here is the documentation for a tool available through OpenAI:
> ## Shell and Skills
> 
> `shell` gives the model a terminal, either in an OpenAI-hosted Debian 12 container or on your own machine. Calls arrive as `shell_call` items (commands, `timeout_ms`, `max_output_length`); hosted runs return `shell_call_output` automatically, local runs need you to send it back.
> 
> ```python
> resp = client.responses.create(
>     model="gpt-6-astra",
>     tools=[{
>         "type": "shell",
>         "environment": {
>             "type": "container_auto",
>             "network_policy": {
>                 "type": "allowlist",
>                 "allowed_domains": ["pypi.org", "files.pythonhosted.org"],
>             },
>             "skills": [{"type": "skill_reference",
>                         "skill_id": "openai-spreadsheets", "version": "latest"}],
>         },
>     }],
>     input="pip install pandas, then summarise /mnt/data/results.csv.",
> )
> ```
> 
> | Option | Values / range | Notes |
> | --- | --- | --- |
> | `environment.type` | `container_auto`, `container_reference`, `local` | Hosted-new, hosted-existing, or your machine |
> | `environment.container_id` | `cntr_...` | With `container_reference` |
> | `environment.network_policy` | `{"type": "allowlist", "allowed_domains": [...], "domain_secrets": [...]}` | No network by default; domains must also be in your org allow list |
> | `domain_secrets[]` | `domain`, `name`, `value` | Model sees only `$NAME`; real value injected for that domain |
> | `environment.skills` | `skill_reference` (`skill_id`, `version` int or `"latest"`) or `inline` (base64 zip) for hosted; `{name, description, path}` for local | Mounts skill bundles |
> 
> Containers created via `/v1/containers` take `name`, `memory_limit`, `expires_after` (`anchor: "last_active_at"`, `minutes`) and `skills`. Hosted runtime ships Python 3.11, Node 22.16, Java 17, PHP 8.2, Ruby 3.1 and Go 1.23; working directory is `/mnt/data`; no TTY or `sudo`.
> 
> **Skills** are folders with a `SKILL.md` manifest, uploaded to `/v1/skills` as multipart or zip. Limits: 50 MB zip, 500 files per version, 25 MB uncompressed per file. OpenAI also offers curated skills such as `openai-spreadsheets`. The older `local_shell` tool is superseded by `shell` with `environment.type: "local"`.
> 
> Can you construct a full tool implementation for this? Please start off the file with a docstring, describing the tool and its usage.
**assistant**:
> ```python
> """
> OpenAI Responses API shell tool adapter.
> 
> This tool asks an OpenAI model to perform a bounded shell-based task using the
> Responses API `shell` tool. Hosted shell environments execute commands inside an
> OpenAI-managed Debian container; returned shell output is preserved as untrusted
> Artifact data rather than injected into prompts or system instructions.
> 
> Usage:
> 
>     tool = make_openai_shell_tool(client, default_model="gpt-6-astra")
> 
>     result = tool.fn(ToolInvocation(
>         operation_id="op_123",
>         conversation_id="conv_123",
>         arguments={
>             "input": "Inspect /mnt/data/results.csv and report its columns.",
>             # Optional: "model": "gpt-6-astra",
>         },
>         config={
>             "environment": {
>                 "type": "container_auto",
>                 "network_policy": {
>                     "type": "allowlist",
>                     "allowed_domains": ["pypi.org"],
>                 },
>             },
>         },
>         context=None,
>         provenance_requirements={},
>     ))
> 
> `local` environments are intentionally unsupported by this adapter: safely
> running model-generated commands on the application host requires a dedicated,
> sandboxed local-shell executor and approval policy.
> """
> 
> from __future__ import annotations
> 
> import json
> from dataclasses import asdict, dataclass, field, is_dataclass
> from typing import Any, Callable, Mapping, Sequence
> 
> # Adjust this import to match your project layout.
> from tools.base import Artifact, Tool, ToolInvocation, ToolResult
> 
> 
> @dataclass(frozen=True)
> class OpenAIShellToolConfig:
>     default_model: str
>     default_environment: Mapping[str, Any] = field(
>         default_factory=lambda: {"type": "container_auto"}
>     )
>     max_input_characters: int = 100_000
>     quality_prior: float | None = None
>     cost_estimate: float | None = None
> 
> 
> class ShellConfigurationError(ValueError):
>     """Raised when shell configuration is invalid or unsafe."""
> 
> 
> def _as_mapping(value: Any, name: str) -> Mapping[str, Any]:
>     if not isinstance(value, Mapping):
>         raise ShellConfigurationError(f"{name} must be an object.")
>     return value
> 
> 
> def _jsonable(value: Any) -> Any:
>     """Best-effort conversion of OpenAI SDK response objects to JSON-like data."""
>     if value is None or isinstance(value, (str, int, float, bool)):
>         return value
>     if isinstance(value, Mapping):
>         return {str(k): _jsonable(v) for k, v in value.items()}
>     if isinstance(value, (list, tuple)):
>         return [_jsonable(v) for v in value]
>     if is_dataclass(value):
>         return _jsonable(asdict(value))
>     if hasattr(value, "model_dump"):
>         return _jsonable(value.model_dump())
>     if hasattr(value, "to_dict"):
>         return _jsonable(value.to_dict())
>     if hasattr(value, "__dict__"):
>         return _jsonable(vars(value))
>     return str(value)
> 
> 
> def _redact_secrets(value: Any, secret_values: Sequence[str]) -> Any:
>     """Avoid persisting configured domain-secret values in returned artifacts."""
>     if isinstance(value, str):
>         for secret in secret_values:
>             if secret:
>                 value = value.replace(secret, "[REDACTED_DOMAIN_SECRET]")
>         return value
>     if isinstance(value, list):
>         return [_redact_secrets(item, secret_values) for item in value]
>     if isinstance(value, dict):
>         return {
>             key: _redact_secrets(item, secret_values)
>             for key, item in value.items()
>         }
>     return value
> 
> 
> def _validate_environment(environment: Mapping[str, Any]) -> dict[str, Any]:
>     environment = dict(environment)
>     environment_type = environment.get("type", "container_auto")
> 
>     if environment_type not in {
>         "container_auto",
>         "container_reference",
>         "local",
>     }:
>         raise ShellConfigurationError(
>             "environment.type must be container_auto, container_reference, or local."
>         )
> 
>     # This adapter deliberately does not execute commands on its own host.
>     if environment_type == "local":
>         raise ShellConfigurationError(
>             "environment.type='local' is unsupported. Use a separately sandboxed "
>             "local-shell executor with explicit command approval."
>         )
> 
>     if environment_type == "container_reference":
>         container_id = environment.get("container_id")
>         if not isinstance(container_id, str) or not container_id.startswith("cntr_"):
>             raise ShellConfigurationError(
>                 "container_reference requires environment.container_id beginning cntr_."
>             )
> 
>     network_policy = environment.get("network_policy")
>     if network_policy is not None:
>         network_policy = _as_mapping(network_policy, "environment.network_policy")
>         if network_policy.get("type") != "allowlist":
>             raise ShellConfigurationError(
>                 "environment.network_policy.type must be 'allowlist'."
>             )
> 
>         domains = network_policy.get("allowed_domains", [])
>         if not isinstance(domains, list) or not all(
>             isinstance(domain, str) and domain for domain in domains
>         ):
>             raise ShellConfigurationError(
>                 "network_policy.allowed_domains must be a list of non-empty strings."
>             )
> 
>         for secret in network_policy.get("domain_secrets", []):
>             secret = _as_mapping(secret, "network_policy.domain_secrets entry")
>             if not all(
>                 isinstance(secret.get(key), str) and secret[key]
>                 for key in ("domain", "name", "value")
>             ):
>                 raise ShellConfigurationError(
>                     "Each domain secret requires non-empty domain, name, and value."
>                 )
> 
>     skills = environment.get("skills", [])
>     if not isinstance(skills, list):
>         raise ShellConfigurationError("environment.skills must be a list.")
> 
>     for skill in skills:
>         skill = _as_mapping(skill, "environment.skills entry")
>         skill_type = skill.get("type")
>         if skill_type == "skill_reference":
>             if not isinstance(skill.get("skill_id"), str):
>                 raise ShellConfigurationError(
>                     "skill_reference requires a string skill_id."
>                 )
>         elif skill_type == "inline":
>             if not isinstance(skill.get("data"), str):
>                 raise ShellConfigurationError(
>                     "inline skill requires base64 zip data in its data field."
>                 )
>         else:
>             raise ShellConfigurationError(
>                 "Hosted shell skills must be skill_reference or inline."
>             )
> 
>     return environment
> 
> 
> def _extract_secret_values(environment: Mapping[str, Any]) -> list[str]:
>     policy = environment.get("network_policy", {})
>     if not isinstance(policy, Mapping):
>         return []
>     secrets = policy.get("domain_secrets", [])
>     if not isinstance(secrets, list):
>         return []
>     return [
>         item["value"]
>         for item in secrets
>         if isinstance(item, Mapping) and isinstance(item.get("value"), str)
>     ]
> 
> 
> def _response_items(response: Any) -> list[dict[str, Any]]:
>     raw = _jsonable(response)
>     if isinstance(raw, Mapping):
>         output = raw.get("output", [])
>         return output if isinstance(output, list) else []
>     return []
> 
> 
> def _artifact(
>     *,
>     kind: str,
>     name: str,
>     data: Any,
>     metadata: Mapping[str, Any],
> ) -> Artifact:
>     """
>     Centralize adaptation if your Artifact constructor uses different field names.
> 
>     This assumes the common Artifact(data=..., mime_type=..., metadata=...) shape.
>     """
>     return Artifact(
>         name=name,
>         kind=kind,
>         data=data,
>         mime_type="application/json",
>         metadata=dict(metadata),
>     )
> 
> 
> def make_openai_shell_tool(
>     client: Any,
>     *,
>     default_model: str,
>     default_environment: Mapping[str, Any] | None = None,
>     quality_prior: float | None = None,
>     cost_estimate: float | None = None,
> ) -> Tool:
>     """
>     Create a synchronous Tool backed by OpenAI's hosted Responses shell runtime.
> 
>     `client` is an initialized OpenAI client exposing `client.responses.create`.
>     """
>     settings = OpenAIShellToolConfig(
>         default_model=default_model,
>         default_environment=(
>             dict(default_environment)
>             if default_environment is not None
>             else {"type": "container_auto"}
>         ),
>         quality_prior=quality_prior,
>         cost_estimate=cost_estimate,
>     )
> 
>     def invoke(invocation: ToolInvocation) -> ToolResult:
>         arguments = _as_mapping(invocation.arguments, "arguments")
>         supplied_config = _as_mapping(invocation.config, "config")
> 
>         user_input = arguments.get("input")
>         if not isinstance(user_input, str) or not user_input.strip():
>             raise ShellConfigurationError("arguments.input must be a non-empty string.")
>         if len(user_input) > settings.max_input_characters:
>             raise ShellConfigurationError(
>                 f"arguments.input exceeds {settings.max_input_characters} characters."
>             )
> 
>         model = arguments.get("model", supplied_config.get("model", settings.default_model))
>         if not isinstance(model, str) or not model:
>             raise ShellConfigurationError("model must be a non-empty string.")
> 
>         environment_config = supplied_config.get(
>             "environment",
>             settings.default_environment,
>         )
>         environment = _validate_environment(
>             _as_mapping(environment_config, "config.environment")
>         )
>         secret_values = _extract_secret_values(environment)
> 
>         # Shell output is external/untrusted data. It is returned only as artifacts.
>         response = client.responses.create(
>             model=model,
>             input=user_input,
>             tools=[{"type": "shell", "environment": environment}],
>         )
> 
>         raw_response = _redact_secrets(_jsonable(response), secret_values)
>         items = _response_items(response)
> 
>         shell_calls = [
>             item for item in items if item.get("type") == "shell_call"
>         ]
>         shell_outputs = [
>             item for item in items if item.get("type") == "shell_call_output"
>         ]
> 
>         artifacts = [
>             _artifact(
>                 kind="openai_shell_response",
>                 name="openai-shell-response.json",
>                 data=raw_response,
>                 metadata={
>                     "operation_id": invocation.operation_id,
>                     "conversation_id": invocation.conversation_id,
>                     "model": model,
>                     "untrusted": True,
>                 },
>             )
>         ]
> 
>         for index, output in enumerate(shell_outputs):
>             artifacts.append(
>                 _artifact(
>                     kind="openai_shell_output",
>                     name=f"shell-output-{index}.json",
>                     data=output,
>                     metadata={
>                         "operation_id": invocation.operation_id,
>                         "conversation_id": invocation.conversation_id,
>                         "untrusted": True,
>                     },
>                 )
>             )
> 
>         return ToolResult(
>             artifacts=artifacts,
>             metadata={
>                 "model": model,
>                 "environment_type": environment["type"],
>                 "shell_call_count": len(shell_calls),
>                 "shell_output_count": len(shell_outputs),
>                 "response_id": raw_response.get("id")
>                 if isinstance(raw_response, Mapping)
>                 else None,
>                 "untrusted_output": True,
>             },
>         )
> 
>     return Tool(
>         name="openai_shell",
>         description=(
>             "Runs a task through OpenAI's hosted shell environment and returns "
>             "untrusted structured shell outputs as artifacts."
>         ),
>         fn=invoke,
>         config=settings,
>         quality_prior=quality_prior,
>         cost_estimate=cost_estimate,
>         version="1",
>     )
> ```
> 
> This intentionally refuses `local` shell execution; model-generated host commands should only run through a dedicated sandbox and approval boundary.
